227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Katalin Kariko, a Hungarian-born biochemist, revolutionized biotechnology by developing nucleoside-modified mRNA, which prevents immune reactions while enabling cells to produce therapeutic proteins. Prior to 2005, the biggest weakness in the application of mRNA, which had therapeutic potential, was that it caused an inflammatory response when administered inside a living organism. In a series of groundbreaking studies initiated in 2005, scientists discovered that although the synthesized mRNA produced   a   high   inflammatory  response.  Kariko  in  collaboration   with    Drew Weissman, discovered that replacing uridine with pseudouridine in mRNA suppressed immune activation, enabling a safe and effective mRNA-based therapies.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [13 14]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 93 0 R
/T (By 2020, her modified mRNA technology formed the basis of the Pfizer-BioNTech and Moderna COVID-19 vaccines, which enabled rapid global vaccination. Beyond COVID-19, her discoveries advanced cancer therapies, rare disease treatments, and personalized medicine, establishing mRNA as a safe, versatile, and scalable platform that has saved millions of lives and transformed modern medicine. In 2023, she was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for her groundbreaking contributions to mRNA science and modern medicine)
/E (By 2020, her modified mRNA technology formed the basis of the Pfizer-BioNTech and Moderna COVID-19 vaccines, which enabled rapid global vaccination. Beyond COVID-19, her discoveries advanced cancer therapies, rare disease treatments, and personalized medicine, establishing mRNA as a safe, versatile, and scalable platform that has saved millions of lives and transformed modern medicine. In 2023, she was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for her groundbreaking contributions to mRNA science and modern medicine)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [15 16]
>>
